WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.667
-Our next guest
is Tony-nominated

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.133
and Grammy Award-winning actor.

00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:05.801
He's currently
starring on Broadway

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:07.100
in "Gutenberg! The Musical!",

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.167
now playing at
the James Earl Jones Theatre.

00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:10.334
Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:12.334
our friend Andrew Rannells,
everybody.

00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:14.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.334 --> 00:00:24.334
♪♪

00:00:24.334 --> 00:00:30.834
♪♪

00:00:30.834 --> 00:00:33.400
Welcome back!
-Hi!

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.734
It feels like it's been
a very long time

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:37.133
since I've been here.
Hi, Syd.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:40.834
-I think, in person, 2019.
So it has been a bit of time.

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:42.334
-There's been --
Yes, I saw photos...

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:43.534
-Yeah.
-...of this very thing.

00:00:43.534 --> 00:00:45.000
-And we looked great.
-We did look great.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.901
We look the same.
-And we look the same now.

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:48.567
-Nothing has changed.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:51.434
-I had your friend and co-star
Josh Gad on the show.

00:00:51.434 --> 00:00:52.901
You seem upset
that he was here.

00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:54.701
-No, no, no, no, I just kind
of thought this was my thing.

00:00:54.701 --> 00:00:55.767
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:58.133
And, you know,
he's, like, an L.A. guy,

00:00:58.133 --> 00:00:59.634
and I was like,
"This is sort of my show."

00:00:59.634 --> 00:01:01.834
-Oh, yeah. Like,
you're kind of a Seth guest.

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:02.901
And he's kind of like
an out-there guest.

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:04.667
-Somebody. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:05.968
It was --
-But it was fine.

00:01:05.968 --> 00:01:07.534
That was nice of you
to be so nice to him.

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.667
-Yes.
-I appreciate that.

00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:11.133
He's very fragile.
-Yeah.

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.033
And L.A. people, like,
they scare easily.

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:15.000
-Oh, my God.
They're super scared.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.133
But, yeah, I really did
drag him --

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:19.334
Because that was his first time
doing your show,

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:22.000
and I was like,
"Oh, I've done it like 10,"

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.033
which I don't think is true,
but it's probably close.

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:26.033
-It's pretty close.
You'd round it up to 10.

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:28.000
-Yeah. Yeah.
-He was talking about --

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.667
And we were
talking about Broadway

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:31.100
and tiny dressing rooms.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.300
-Yes, we do have
tiny dressing rooms.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.734
-The proximity
of dressing rooms.

00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:34.934
-We are right
next to each other.

00:01:34.934 --> 00:01:37.067
You would think maybe
we would have gotten

00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:38.801
a little bit farther apart,
but, no, that's not the case.

00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:40.133
-Because the entire show
is the two of you

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.300
on stage together.
-It's just the two of us.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.834
We have a three-person band
on stage with us,

00:01:43.834 --> 00:01:45.501
and just Josh and I.

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:47.734
So we're in this
little space together.

00:01:47.734 --> 00:01:49.667
-He was talking about
your warm-up before the show.

00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:51.300
Do you warm up
in the same way or --

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.767
-We do not.
No, Josh, um...

00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:55.067
Josh's vocal warm-up

00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:57.801
is, like, mostly like '80s,
like, power ballads.

00:01:57.801 --> 00:02:00.467
-Yeah.
-And I stick to more sort of

00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:04.200
traditional, like, musical
theater things to warm up to.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.467
But recently,
it's been very strange.

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:10.334
Our dresser, Alex Bartlett,
and I have been very confused.

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:12.100
Sometimes during intermission,
Josh will go into

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.267
his dressing room and listen to,
like, acoustic versions of

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:20.033
"Everybody Hurts" or "Creep"
or, like --

00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:21.968
Like, it's really --
it gets real dark,

00:02:21.968 --> 00:02:23.801
and we're like, "Joshy..."
-And that's a very counter

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:25.067
energy to the play.
-No, yeah.

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:27.801
This show is like --
it's, like, all high energy.

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:29.567
And Josh is in there like,

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:31.667
I guess,
slowly rocking in the dark.

00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:33.601
I don't know what he's doing.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:34.901
But I'm concerned.
-They are long days

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:36.234
when you have double shows.

00:02:36.234 --> 00:02:38.300
He was saying that he will ta--
he will lie on a --

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.467
He will take a nap on a cot

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:41.834
that you have described
as his FEMA cot.

00:02:41.834 --> 00:02:43.734
-Well, it is
a little jarring.

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:46.167
Usually --
Yeah, I mean, I'm all for naps.

00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:47.334
Don't get me wrong.

00:02:47.334 --> 00:02:49.868
But he does sleep
like sort of an infant.

00:02:49.868 --> 00:02:52.367
And he sleeps wherever
and whenever he can.

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:54.868
So they have, like,
a full rollaway bed

00:02:54.868 --> 00:02:57.267
that he pulls into his room
from the hallway

00:02:57.267 --> 00:02:58.734
and just lays down and, I guess,

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:01.000
cries himself to sleep.
I don't know.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.701
-Due to his acoustic --
his acoustic sad-song mix.

00:03:03.701 --> 00:03:06.167
-Yeah, but he takes --
he takes a lot of naps.

00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:07.434
I mean, naps are important.

00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:08.767
Broadway is hard.
-Broadway is hard.

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:11.667
-Eight shows. You know,
it's a very active show.

00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:13.133
It's just the two of us.
-Just the two of you.

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:15.734
Now, one nice thing, though,
is, the audience

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:18.067
doesn't play a role, but there's
no fourth wall in the show.

00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:19.601
So you get to talk
to the audience.

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:21.901
-Which is a good thing
and a bad thing, as you know.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.801
Like, opening the door
to the audience of, like,

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:26.601
there's sort of a false sense
of, like,

00:03:26.601 --> 00:03:28.667
"Do we participate?"
and the answer is no.

00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:29.667
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.667 --> 00:03:31.601
-They're there to be spoken to.

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:33.801
-You do not get to participate.

00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:37.634
So -- Well, you know,
we are introducing ourselves,

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:39.801
and we're explaining the show
and -- and, you know,

00:03:39.801 --> 00:03:42.567
most of the time,
most audiences are fantastic.

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:44.934
And then some audiences,
we get some -- like, some jokers

00:03:44.934 --> 00:03:47.934
who are like, "Hey, Olaf!"

00:03:47.934 --> 00:03:50.734
You know,
and they do some like...

00:03:50.734 --> 00:03:52.567
"That's not what
we're doing, sir."

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:54.968
-And do you just basically
have the -- have the permission

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:56.467
to say that to them?

00:03:56.467 --> 00:03:57.968
-Kind of. Yeah.
In this show.

00:03:57.968 --> 00:04:00.400
I mean, I --

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.234
Yes, I have, like,
a little bit more --

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:04.534
What's the best way to say this?

00:04:04.534 --> 00:04:05.801
A tiny, more experience

00:04:05.801 --> 00:04:08.434
in front of a live audience
than Josh does.

00:04:08.434 --> 00:04:10.067
This is Josh's first time back.

00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:11.601
I'm talking about him
like he's dead.

00:04:11.601 --> 00:04:13.801
But he's -- it's his first time
back to Broadway

00:04:13.801 --> 00:04:15.367
since "The Book of Mormon."
-Yeah.

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:17.868
-Whoo!
-And -- thank you.

00:04:17.868 --> 00:04:21.167
And, Josh --

00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:23.033
You know,
the general rule of improv,

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:25.434
Seth, is, "Yes, and..."
-"Yes, and..."

00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:26.701
-Correct?
-Whatever your partner says,

00:04:26.701 --> 00:04:27.934
you got to roll with it.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:30.934
-So, you know,
inevitably things will go wrong

00:04:30.934 --> 00:04:33.133
in a Broadway show,
and in this show,

00:04:33.133 --> 00:04:35.434
we sort of differentiate
all these different characters

00:04:35.434 --> 00:04:38.033
we play by wearing these hats

00:04:38.033 --> 00:04:39.367
with the characters' names
on them,

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:42.334
and, usually,
everything goes fine.

00:04:42.334 --> 00:04:43.667
And sometimes there's
a little --

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:45.033
you know,
there can be a little kerfuffle

00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:46.467
or, you know, it's a new show.

00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:48.467
We were in previews, so there
are some changes and stuff.

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:50.434
So, one night,
we were doing the show,

00:04:50.434 --> 00:04:53.567
and I skipped like four pages.

00:04:53.567 --> 00:04:55.767
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:57.868
Like, four full pages.

00:04:57.868 --> 00:05:00.267
And -- But in my mind,
I was thinking about it,

00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:05.033
and I was like, "Well, I could
try to go back, but why?"

00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:07.701
And I was like, "It's probably
for the better for everyone,

00:05:07.701 --> 00:05:10.133
for the crew.
Everyone wants to go home."

00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:14.100
So I just kind of kept going
and just was like,

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.634
"We'll just
skip the four pages."

00:05:15.634 --> 00:05:17.133
-And were you making
eye contact with him

00:05:17.133 --> 00:05:19.167
as if to be like,
"I acknowledge I missed it,

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:20.834
but let's keep going"?
-Well, that's on me.

00:05:20.834 --> 00:05:22.367
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:24.567
Perhaps I didn't
do that clearly enough,

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:28.100
but his improv, to me, was,

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.634
"You don't know
where the [bleep] you are."

00:05:29.634 --> 00:05:31.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.367 --> 00:05:32.701
-He said that on stage
for the audience to hear.

00:05:32.701 --> 00:05:33.834
-Yeah.

00:05:33.834 --> 00:05:35.434
-Yeah, that's not good improv
at all.

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:40.200
-No. And to which I replied,
"No, I don't."

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.367
And then we got back on track.

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:43.067
But it was not --
-That's not kind.

00:05:43.067 --> 00:05:44.634
-It was, "No, but..."
-Did he make you go back,

00:05:44.634 --> 00:05:46.434
or did you keep going?
-Oh, I kept going.

00:05:46.434 --> 00:05:47.834
-Yeah, yeah.
You can't then --

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:49.567
-I punished them all.
-You know why you can't?

00:05:49.567 --> 00:05:51.601
Because if you then went back,

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:54.334
the audience would see
exactly how much you'd missed

00:05:54.334 --> 00:05:55.801
when you got back to the place
you were at.

00:05:55.801 --> 00:05:59.701
-And I'll be honest,
I have no clue what I missed.

00:05:59.701 --> 00:06:01.067
And I really did.
I went backstage,

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:02.701
and I was like,
"I know I missed a lot."

00:06:02.701 --> 00:06:03.834
And they were like,
"It's four pages."

00:06:03.834 --> 00:06:05.200
I was like, "Oh, okay.
Well..."

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.234
-You also have a very
cool thing in the show.

00:06:07.234 --> 00:06:08.834
There is a cameo every night.

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:12.100
-Oh, yes. Yes, we do.
We have a special guest cameo.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.100
-And do you -- how far
in advance do you know

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.567
who the cameo will be?

00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:17.434
-Sometimes the day of.
-Gotcha.

00:06:17.434 --> 00:06:19.100
-You know, but we do rehearse
with the person

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:20.200
a little bit before.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.968
So we actually
just had your friend

00:06:22.968 --> 00:06:26.067
and also fellow Omahan
Amber Ruffin.

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:28.567
-Amber Ruffin, the great
Amber Ruffin, in the show.

00:06:28.567 --> 00:06:31.067
-Who killed it.
Was hilarious.

00:06:31.067 --> 00:06:33.601
-You also -- the show is called
"Gutenberg!" and you just had --

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:36.100
-We had Steve Guttenberg.
-You had Steve Guttenberg.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.767
-Steve Guttenberg, spelled
with two T's, in case

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:40.968
anyone's keeping track.

00:06:40.968 --> 00:06:43.701
"Gutenberg" is --
Johann is with one,

00:06:43.701 --> 00:06:46.100
Steve is with two.
No, he was fantastic.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.400
And I -- you know, he was
a huge part of my childhood.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.634
And he's, like, in so many
movies from that time.

00:06:52.634 --> 00:06:55.267
-That you watch on VHS tape
a thousand times each.

00:06:55.267 --> 00:06:57.934
-A thousand times.
Yeah. So that was very exciting.

00:06:57.934 --> 00:06:59.300
-Alex Timbers is the director.
Alex Timbers.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.767
-Wonderful director.
-Fantastic director.

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:03.400
-A collaborative director.
-Very collaborative, yes.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.934
-But yet you've worked
with directors in the past --

00:07:04.934 --> 00:07:06.701
Have you ever received notes
from directors that you were

00:07:06.701 --> 00:07:10.634
maybe trying to be constructive
but rang a little...?

00:07:10.634 --> 00:07:13.400
-Yeah, early on,
I think sometimes they get --

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:14.767
Well,
what am I saying, early on?

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:17.234
Sometimes still today.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.234 --> 00:07:18.901
...get impatient,
and they want to say --

00:07:18.901 --> 00:07:20.033
And I'm all for it.

00:07:20.033 --> 00:07:22.567
Like, if you want it
faster or louder...

00:07:22.567 --> 00:07:23.834
-Yeah.
-...just say faster or louder.

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:26.167
'Cause sometimes people
can get into like,

00:07:26.167 --> 00:07:28.701
"Well, I wonder if there
should be more immediacy."

00:07:28.701 --> 00:07:30.834
You're like, "You mean faster."
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:32.334
"You want it faster?
That's great."

00:07:32.334 --> 00:07:34.200
So I like to cut to the chase.
Don't get me wrong.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:37.234
-Right.
-But I did have a director --

00:07:37.234 --> 00:07:38.734
He gave me --
I used to keep them

00:07:38.734 --> 00:07:41.400
'cause he would write his notes
down and hand them out.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.467
My favorites were
"Quit acting with your hair."

00:07:44.467 --> 00:07:48.133
[ Laughter ]
That's a good one.

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:50.167
"Charming is not a choice."

00:07:50.167 --> 00:07:51.300
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.400
I beg to differ.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.968
I could choose to be a dick
right now, but I'm not.

00:07:55.968 --> 00:07:58.834
And, um -- oh, the other
good one was,

00:07:58.834 --> 00:08:01.067
"Andrew,
you are not James Dean,"

00:08:01.067 --> 00:08:03.300
which I don't really know
how that applied to the show

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.033
we were doing, but I think maybe
he was just tired or confused.

00:08:07.033 --> 00:08:08.834
-He was like, "I got to give him
a note on today."

00:08:08.834 --> 00:08:10.234
-Something.
-"Let him know who he's not."

00:08:10.234 --> 00:08:12.234
-"You're not James Dean." Okay.
-Yeah, I have to say --

00:08:12.234 --> 00:08:13.868
Can I say something?
I think "Book of Mormon"

00:08:13.868 --> 00:08:15.868
would've sucked with James Dean.
-Thank you.

00:08:15.868 --> 00:08:17.033
-Yeah.
-He would've just, like,

00:08:17.033 --> 00:08:19.400
mumbled his way through it.
-Way too broody.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.167
-Not fun at all.
-Really -- Yeah.

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:22.601
So I'm glad I'm not James Dean.
-Yeah.

00:08:22.601 --> 00:08:24.434
-It worked out.
-I'm even happier.

00:08:24.434 --> 00:08:26.033
-But I'm still acting
with my hair, so take that.

00:08:26.033 --> 00:08:28.200
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
And can I say something?

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:29.601
You're very charming
when you do it.

00:08:29.601 --> 00:08:31.167
Yeah, and it's a choice.
-Seth!

00:08:31.167 --> 00:08:33.000
-It's a choice I care for.
-Come on.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.801
-You guys,
that's Andrew Rannells.

00:08:34.801 --> 00:08:37.100
"Gutenberg! The Musical!"
is playing now

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.267
at the James Earl Jones Theatre.
Go see it, and stick around.

00:08:39.267 --> 00:08:41.968
Be right back with Jesmyn Ward.

